{
  "_what": "What ttsim's two instruments measure, derived from the committed simulator logs. This is the evidence that licenses the 2026-09-03 retraction of our Tenstorrent speed claims: neither instrument can support a head-to-head throughput number.",
  "_generated_by": "python3 scripts/accel/audit-ttsim-instrument.py \u2014 never hand-edited",
  "provenance": "derived_from_committed_simulator_logs",
  "logs_parsed": 403,
  "verdict_headline": "ttsim MEASURES NO TIME AND THREE KINDS OF WORK. Every clock on it \u2014 the bracket counter, the host wall clock's usability, and the device's own per-core cycle register \u2014 fails or cannot settle the K sweep. But the instrumented build's counters (Tensix instructions issued, NOC transactions, NOC bytes) rise with the work, and retired matmuls rise EXACTLY with it. So a SPEED claim is still unavailable without silicon, and an EFFICIENCY claim \u2014 same computation, less of the machine spent on it \u2014 is now measurable, checkable, and conserved.",
  "verdict": "NO ttsim INSTRUMENT MEASURES SPEED. The bracket cycle counter does not track work (it is bit-identical across a wide sweep of the reduction depth at fixed configuration); the host wall clock does track work but is far too noisy on a shared machine to resolve a head-to-head margin; and the DEVICE-side per-core counter, which is the natural thing to reach for once the first two are ruled out, is flat in K on the simulator as well. This is a property of the measuring device, not of the emitted program. The device instrumentation is implemented and correct \u2014 it simply needs SILICON to mean anything, because ttsim is a functional model and does not simulate per-core timing. Until then the spatial speed claim lives on the Plasticine/RDU lane, whose Tungsten simulator is a cycle-accurate timing model.",
